From: ps5cemu <ps5cemu@localhost>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 04:24:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: HighResolutionTimer counts nanoseconds on FreeBSD

now() reads CLOCK_MONOTONIC in nanoseconds, but the frequency came from
clock_getres, which FreeBSD gives as 10^9 / the clock's frequency + 1
nanoseconds. The PS5's is 81 ns, so every timer ran 81 times too fast:
the emulated vsync, and through the TSC's frequency, measured against
this timer, the PowerPC's time base. Games ran as fast as the display
let them: The Wind Waker HD at 60 fps, twice its speed. The frequency
is now 10^9, as on macOS.
---
 src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp b/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp
index 6bb96a8..54fa4fb 100644
--- a/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp
+++ b/src/util/highresolutiontimer/HighResolutionTimer.cpp
@@ -34,9 +34,10 @@ uint64 HighResolutionTimer::m_freq = []() -> uint64 {
 #elif BOOST_OS_MACOS
 	return 1000000000;
 #elif BOOST_OS_BSD
-	timespec pc;
-	clock_getres(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &pc);
-	return (uint64)1000000000 / (uint64)pc.tv_nsec;
+	// now() counts nanoseconds, whatever the clock's resolution: FreeBSD's clock_getres gives
+	// that as 10^9 / the clock's frequency + 1 nanoseconds, so with a clock of 1 GHz or less
+	// every timer ran that many times too fast (81 on the PS5)
+	return 1000000000;
 #else
     timespec pc;
     clock_getres(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW, &pc);
